 const DATA={"scenarios":[{"title":"The unanswered message","scene":"You sent a friend an important message yesterday. They have been active online but have not answered. You feel overlooked.","question":"What would you do next?","choices":[{"text":"They may have seen it, so I will give them another day before deciding what it means.","rating":"Reasonable alternative","why":"Waiting may help, but the fact that they were online does not establish why they have not replied.","points":1,"feedback":"Waiting may help, but the fact that they were online does not establish why they have not replied. Another approach: “I feel overlooked, but I do not know why they have not replied. I can follow up directly if needed.” You recognize your emotion, separate it from an unverified motive, and keep a practical next step available.","best":false},{"text":"I feel overlooked, but I do not know why they have not replied. I can follow up directly if needed.","rating":"Strongest response","why":"You recognize your emotion, separate it from an unverified motive, and keep a practical next step available.","points":2,"feedback":"You recognize your emotion, separate it from an unverified motive, and keep a practical next step available.","best":true},{"text":"I know they are busy sometimes, but I should probably lower my expectations of this friendship.","rating":"Partly effective","why":"Adjusting expectations can be healthy after a pattern, but one delay is too little information for a firm conclusion.","points":1,"feedback":"Adjusting expectations can be healthy after a pattern, but one delay is too little information for a firm conclusion. Another approach: “I feel overlooked, but I do not know why they have not replied. I can follow up directly if needed.” You recognize your emotion, separate it from an unverified motive, and keep a practical next step available.","best":false}]},{"title":"The look across the room","scene":"At a family gathering, someone looks at you briefly and turns away. You remember a disagreement from last month and feel uneasy.","question":"What is the most careful conclusion?","choices":[{"text":"The disagreement may still matter to them, so I will keep my distance tonight.","rating":"Partly effective","why":"Giving space can be appropriate, but you are still making an unverified assumption about what the glance means.","points":1,"feedback":"Giving space can be appropriate, but you are still making an unverified assumption about what the glance means. Another approach: “I noticed the glance and felt uneasy. I cannot know their intention from that alone.” You acknowledge your reaction while avoiding mind-reading. If the relationship matters, you can speak directly later.","best":false},{"text":"They might be uncomfortable, but I will ask another relative whether they are upset.","rating":"Partly effective","why":"Seeking context can help, but asking third parties may invite speculation and gossip.","points":1,"feedback":"Seeking context can help, but asking third parties may invite speculation and gossip. Another approach: “I noticed the glance and felt uneasy. I cannot know their intention from that alone.” You acknowledge your reaction while avoiding mind-reading. If the relationship matters, you can speak directly later.","best":false},{"text":"I noticed the glance and felt uneasy. I cannot know their intention from that alone.","rating":"Strongest response","why":"You acknowledge your reaction while avoiding mind-reading. If the relationship matters, you can speak directly later.","points":2,"feedback":"You acknowledge your reaction while avoiding mind-reading. If the relationship matters, you can speak directly later.","best":true}]},{"title":"The missed deadline","scene":"A coworker promised to send information by noon. It is now 3 PM, and you need it to finish a report.","question":"What would you say to your coworker?","choices":[{"text":"“I was expecting the information by noon. What is the updated timing? I need it to finish the report.”","rating":"Strongest response","why":"You identify the observable delay, state the impact, and request the information needed to act.","points":2,"feedback":"You identify the observable delay, state the impact, and request the information needed to act.","best":true},{"text":"“I know things come up, but it would help if you warned me earlier next time.”","rating":"Reasonable alternative","why":"This may improve future communication, but it does not directly address when you will receive the information now.","points":1,"feedback":"This may improve future communication, but it does not directly address when you will receive the information now. Another approach: “I was expecting the information by noon. What is the updated timing? I need it to finish the report.” You identify the observable delay, state the impact, and request the information needed to act.","best":false},{"text":"“I’ll finish the other sections first. Let me know when you have the information.”","rating":"Partly effective","why":"Working on what you can control is sensible, but avoiding a direct follow-up may prolong the blockage.","points":1,"feedback":"Working on what you can control is sensible, but avoiding a direct follow-up may prolong the blockage. Another approach: “I was expecting the information by noon. What is the updated timing? I need it to finish the report.” You identify the observable delay, state the impact, and request the information needed to act.","best":false}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your friend has not replied. Which statement is a fact?","choices":["Your friend has been online but has not answered your message.","Your friend saw the message and decided you were unimportant.","Your friend is avoiding a difficult conversation."],"correct":0,"why":"Online activity and an unanswered message can be observed, but they do not establish why someone has not replied.","feedbacks":["Online activity and an unanswered message are observable; neither proves a motive.","This assumes the friend saw your message and judged your importance, neither of which you know.","Avoidance is one possible explanation, but you have not established it as fact."]},{"q":"A glance reminds you of a previous disagreement. What is the strongest interpretation?","choices":["The person is probably still angry, so you should protect yourself.","Feeling uneasy is real, but a glance alone does not reveal what someone thinks.","Their silence proves they have moved on."],"correct":1,"why":"Your feeling is real, but a brief glance does not reveal another person's thoughts or intentions.","feedbacks":["Past disagreement can color your interpretation; a glance alone does not establish continued anger.","You acknowledge your feeling without presenting your interpretation as fact.","Silence does not prove someone has moved on; you would still be guessing."]},{"q":"A coworker misses a deadline. What would you say to request an update without assuming a motive?","choices":["“You seem overwhelmed, so I will wait until you are ready.”","“I will tell my supervisor you are unreliable.”","“The noon deadline passed. When can I expect the information I need?”"],"correct":2,"why":"A factual, direct request addresses the problem without assuming the cause. Strongest answer: “The noon deadline passed. When can I expect the information I need?”","feedbacks":["Waiting may be considerate, but it assumes overwhelm and leaves your work blocked without a clear plan.","Calling someone unreliable makes a character judgment before clarifying what happened.","You describe the missed deadline and request a concrete update without assigning motive."]}]};
